# Proposed exact transport of nine completed evidence files

All nine originals are still present. The eligible set is one completed accepted 1,581 ownership audit and the eight completed imported-artifact ledgers of accepted 1568, 1570, 1575, 1581 and separate 219, smoothness, 249 and 242 profiles. Original failed attempts, native traces, mathematical sources, compiler objects, all RESULT/final receipts and every other file are excluded.

The raw total is 189,685,299 bytes. New deterministic ledger gzip archives consume 11,700,307 bytes. The 1,581 audit reuses its already published and fully read-back exact ordered gzip parts. Every original byte has been compared with recovered archive bytes. No representation change has occurred. Expected net recovery relative to the pre-preparation state is about 178 MB, to be measured rather than assumed. This should restore the unchanged 20 GiB launch reserve for a small selector profile, subject to final preparation and invocation checks.

The code preserves the prior reviewed archive/restore/fsync, advisory-lock, inode identity, journal, no-overwrite and explicit-reader contracts. The allowlist expands to exactly nine pinned paths. The distinct imported_artifact_ledger binding verifies the same completed target/run, exact unchanged PASS RESULT and final-receipt hashes, exact newly frozen raw digest/length, and archive/manifest/part digests. Where five earlier independent final reports serialized a ledger digest, those exact reports and named fields are also checked. The old RESULT did not itself serialize a digest for these ledger files; this proposal does not invent that historical binding. The other three ledger digests are newly pinned original-byte identities reviewed here. The ownership audit retains its original auxiliary-output receipt binding.

The operation requires a quiescent workspace. Advisory locks and path identities are not protection against a hostile concurrent replacement. Legacy raw readers must explicitly restore the bytes or call the reviewed read_evidence adapter; there is no implicit missing-file fallback or global filesystem patch. Raw RESULT files remain available to all earlier historical audit adapters. The compiler and cache verifier are unchanged.

Executed synthetic checks cover the inherited thirteen controls, plus nine ledger-specific target/run/receipt/review-digest and roundtrip cases. Only synthetic fixture bytes were mutated. Independent review is required before activating any real archive. Restore requires available disk space and refuses overwriting conflicting bytes.

Next proposed consumer is the separate 262-module G4 COMMON prefix selector check (261 exact checked prerequisites plus one statically reviewed draft). No launch, graph acceptance, theorem closure or durable disk enlargement follows from this transport alone.
